INSERT INTO sat_questions (question_id, external_id, vault_id, disclosed_item_id, source, program, test_type, test_type_desc, domain, primary_class_cd, skill_cd, skill_desc, difficulty, score_band_range_cd, question_type, stimulus_html, stem_html, answer_options, correct_answer, answer_keys, rationale_html, correct_answer_extracted_from_rationale, image_count, parent_template_name, parent_template_id, template_cluster_id, template_cluster_name, origin, position, answer_style, section, raw_payload, created_date, updated_date) VALUES
    ($SATQ$42301836$SATQ$, $SATQ$f0168aca-49ba-46ea-984f-c7b7cff2bd53$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In her <span data-ssml-say-as="date">2012</span> analysis of tree rings from Japan&rsquo;s Yaku Island, cosmic ray physicist Fusa Miyake noted an anomalous carbon-14 spike dating to 774&ndash;775 CE, indicating that a massive burst of radiation reached Earth during that time. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> this unprecedented radiocarbon surge was dubbed a &ldquo;Miyake event&rdquo; in honor of its discoverer.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4e58bca7-9471-4f74-bcee-d789f02717f1","content":"<p>Fittingly,</p>"},{"id":"4c60c32c-3590-4ff9-840a-c88522fafb82","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"5ba4b82e-65d7-4c1f-91d7-2691e34c2579","content":"<p>However,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"965d1b03-b838-44b6-ad47-bd84a1a9803e","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4e58bca7-9471-4f74-bcee-d789f02717f1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. "Fittingly" logically signals that the naming of an unprecedented radiocarbon surge for Fusa Miyake is appropriate to the situation, since Miyake is the person who identified the surge (through her Yaku Island tree-ring analysis).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because "similarly" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the previous information about Miyake&rsquo;s identification of a massive radiation burst through tree-ring analysis. Instead, the naming of the event for its discoverer is a fitting and appropriate outcome. Choice C is incorrect because "however" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the previous information about Miyake&rsquo;s identification of a massive radiation burst through tree-ring analysis. Instead, the naming of the event for its discoverer is a fitting and appropriate outcome. Choice D is incorrect because "in other words" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about Miyake&rsquo;s identification of a massive radiation burst through tree-ring analysis.  Instead, the naming of the event for its discoverer is a fitting and appropriate outcome.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"42301836","external_id":"f0168aca-49ba-46ea-984f-c7b7cff2bd53","vaultid":null,"uId":"34d13ae4-26c2-4e89-8ed4-4e0c3331811d","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In her <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2012</span> analysis of tree rings from Japan&rsquo;s Yaku Island, cosmic ray physicist Fusa Miyake noted an anomalous carbon-14 spike dating to 774&ndash;775 CE, indicating that a massive burst of radiation reached Earth during that time. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> this unprecedented radiocarbon surge was dubbed a &ldquo;Miyake event&rdquo; in honor of its discoverer.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4e58bca7-9471-4f74-bcee-d789f02717f1","content":"<p>Fittingly,</p>"},{"id":"4c60c32c-3590-4ff9-840a-c88522fafb82","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"5ba4b82e-65d7-4c1f-91d7-2691e34c2579","content":"<p>However,&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"965d1b03-b838-44b6-ad47-bd84a1a9803e","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"}],"keys":["4e58bca7-9471-4f74-bcee-d789f02717f1"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. \"Fittingly\" logically signals that the naming of an unprecedented radiocarbon surge for Fusa Miyake is appropriate to the situation, since Miyake is the person who identified the surge (through her Yaku Island tree-ring analysis).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because \"similarly\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the previous information about Miyake&rsquo;s identification of a massive radiation burst through tree-ring analysis. Instead, the naming of the event for its discoverer is a fitting and appropriate outcome. Choice C is incorrect because \"however\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the previous information about Miyake&rsquo;s identification of a massive radiation burst through tree-ring analysis. Instead, the naming of the event for its discoverer is a fitting and appropriate outcome. Choice D is incorrect because \"in other words\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a paraphrase or restatement of the previous information about Miyake&rsquo;s identification of a massive radiation burst through tree-ring analysis.  Instead, the naming of the event for its discoverer is a fitting and appropriate outcome.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669215,"updateDate":1743430669215}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669215, 1743430669215),
    ($SATQ$42870a4e$SATQ$, $SATQ$f3b8d7b1-cf14-41bd-a7c7-78aa3b590506$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Marcel Duchamp intended his 1917 so-called ready-made sculpture <em>Fountain</em> to challenge then-prevailing conceptions about the nature of art. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Duchamp&rsquo;s <em>Fountain</em> did just that, raising the question of whether displaying any object in an art gallery could be said to transform the object&mdash;even, as Duchamp&rsquo;s sculpture was, a urinal&mdash;into a legitimate work of art.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cc220360-2197-4d4d-a22c-d8b66fdd829b","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"5f57c6b4-47f5-4c81-a1c0-2984d7819852","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"3b781b61-cadd-4e78-9382-8b9038fa9ea7","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"4fec5166-e9c5-4563-8702-56d0e553889e","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["5f57c6b4-47f5-4c81-a1c0-2984d7819852"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "Indeed" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the sculpture raised the question of whether displaying any object in an art gallery transforms the object into a work of art&mdash;offers emphasis in support of the claim in the previous sentence that the sculpture was intended to challenge conceptions about the nature of art.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "similarly" illogically signals that information in this sentence is similar to the claim about the sculptor&rsquo;s intention in the previous sentence. Instead, the information about the question raised by the sculpture offers emphasis in support of that claim. Choice C is incorrect because "instead" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an alternative to the claim about the sculptor&rsquo;s intention in the previous sentence. Rather, the information about the question raised by the sculpture offers emphasis in support of that claim. Choice D is incorrect because "in addition" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact related to the claim about the sculptor&rsquo;s intention in the previous sentence. Instead, the information about the question raised by the sculpture offers emphasis in support of that claim.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"42870a4e","external_id":"f3b8d7b1-cf14-41bd-a7c7-78aa3b590506","vaultid":null,"uId":"62344767-f087-476d-983d-41983f9c3e95","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Marcel Duchamp intended his 1917 so-called ready-made sculpture <em>Fountain</em> to challenge then-prevailing conceptions about the nature of art. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Duchamp&rsquo;s <em>Fountain</em> did just that, raising the question of whether displaying any object in an art gallery could be said to transform the object&mdash;even, as Duchamp&rsquo;s sculpture was, a urinal&mdash;into a legitimate work of art.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cc220360-2197-4d4d-a22c-d8b66fdd829b","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"5f57c6b4-47f5-4c81-a1c0-2984d7819852","content":"<p>Indeed,</p>"},{"id":"3b781b61-cadd-4e78-9382-8b9038fa9ea7","content":"<p>Instead,</p>"},{"id":"4fec5166-e9c5-4563-8702-56d0e553889e","content":"<p>In addition,</p>"}],"keys":["5f57c6b4-47f5-4c81-a1c0-2984d7819852"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"Indeed\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that the sculpture raised the question of whether displaying any object in an art gallery transforms the object into a work of art&mdash;offers emphasis in support of the claim in the previous sentence that the sculpture was intended to challenge conceptions about the nature of art.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"similarly\" illogically signals that information in this sentence is similar to the claim about the sculptor&rsquo;s intention in the previous sentence. Instead, the information about the question raised by the sculpture offers emphasis in support of that claim. Choice C is incorrect because \"instead\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an alternative to the claim about the sculptor&rsquo;s intention in the previous sentence. Rather, the information about the question raised by the sculpture offers emphasis in support of that claim. Choice D is incorrect because \"in addition\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is merely an additional fact related to the claim about the sculptor&rsquo;s intention in the previous sentence. Instead, the information about the question raised by the sculpture offers emphasis in support of that claim.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669215,"updateDate":1743430669215}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669215, 1743430669215),
    ($SATQ$42d92dea$SATQ$, $SATQ$b8087e41-58a8-47b7-8929-ef5d9f082f0d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 2, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Before it unveiled a massive new gallery in 2009, the Art Institute of Chicago was only able to display about 5% of its art collection. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the museum is able to display close to 30% of its collection.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"6eb0eec2-67c8-487a-90ed-013357c650ce","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"8b7c440c-4a9e-4287-97ab-abec7d6a7e2a","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"c16658c0-9c95-4a37-ab82-5a488dcb5363","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"1aae503a-f2bb-4508-93c5-3c834b0cfb82","content":"<p>Today,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1aae503a-f2bb-4508-93c5-3c834b0cfb82"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. "Today" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that the museum is able to display close to 30% of its collection&mdash;is true of the Art Institute of Chicago as it exists in the present day after the previously mentioned unveiling of the massive new gallery in 2009.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "additionally" illogically signals that the information in the sentence is merely an additional fact related to the information about the museum before the new gallery opened. Instead, the sentence is about the museum in the present day after the new gallery opened. Choice B is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that the information in the sentence exemplifies the previous information about the museum before the new gallery opened. Instead, the sentence is about the museum in the present day after the new gallery opened. Choice C is incorrect because "nevertheless" illogically signals that the information in the sentence is true despite the previous information about the museum before the new gallery opened. Instead, the sentence is about the museum in the present day after the new gallery opened.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"42d92dea","external_id":"b8087e41-58a8-47b7-8929-ef5d9f082f0d","vaultid":null,"uId":"e5f3ba2c-1070-4bd1-9e6b-fcdcc5a985fd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":2,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Before it unveiled a massive new gallery in 2009, the Art Institute of Chicago was only able to display about 5% of its art collection. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the museum is able to display close to 30% of its collection.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"6eb0eec2-67c8-487a-90ed-013357c650ce","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"8b7c440c-4a9e-4287-97ab-abec7d6a7e2a","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"c16658c0-9c95-4a37-ab82-5a488dcb5363","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"1aae503a-f2bb-4508-93c5-3c834b0cfb82","content":"<p>Today,</p>"}],"keys":["1aae503a-f2bb-4508-93c5-3c834b0cfb82"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. \"Today\" logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that the museum is able to display close to 30% of its collection&mdash;is true of the Art Institute of Chicago as it exists in the present day after the previously mentioned unveiling of the massive new gallery in 2009.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"additionally\" illogically signals that the information in the sentence is merely an additional fact related to the information about the museum before the new gallery opened. Instead, the sentence is about the museum in the present day after the new gallery opened. Choice B is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that the information in the sentence exemplifies the previous information about the museum before the new gallery opened. Instead, the sentence is about the museum in the present day after the new gallery opened. Choice C is incorrect because \"nevertheless\" illogically signals that the information in the sentence is true despite the previous information about the museum before the new gallery opened. Instead, the sentence is about the museum in the present day after the new gallery opened.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323042,"updateDate":1730147323042}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323042, 1730147323042),
    ($SATQ$42e6cc83$SATQ$, $SATQ$475f0dde-a4ec-4f91-b2b4-de36e7b1a6ca$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In hindsight, given the ideas about the natural world circulating among British scientists in the 1800s, the theory of natural selection was an obvious next step. It may not have been a coincidence, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> that Charles Darwin and Alfred Wallace arrived at the concept independently. Indeed, contrary to the popular myth of the lone genius, theirs is not the first paradigm-shifting theory to have emerged from multiple scholars working in parallel.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"125507ea-2aaa-46bf-9a16-d0f0a95debd0","content":"<p>however,</p>"},{"id":"8a170ec2-1a89-4848-b325-cb531038e4ce","content":"<p>then,</p>"},{"id":"34184b1e-95cd-45c2-82fe-4050e2a0c521","content":"<p>moreover,</p>"},{"id":"fae5e2f9-d60c-479e-8800-ebf89ae51ec7","content":"<p>for example,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["8a170ec2-1a89-4848-b325-cb531038e4ce"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "Then" signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about Darwin and Wallace follows logically from the previous information. In other words, both scientists independently arriving at the theory of natural selection was, arguably, an expected outcome of the circumstances mentioned in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "however" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence contrasts with the previous information about the ideas circulating among British scientists in the 1800s. Instead, this claim follows logically from that information. Choice C is incorrect because "moreover" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence merely adds to the previous information about the ideas circulating among British scientists in the 1800s. Instead, this claim follows logically from that information. Choice D is incorrect because "for example" illogically signals that this sentence provides an example supporting the previous information about the ideas circulating among British scientists in the 1800s. Instead, it presents a claim that follows logically from that information.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"42e6cc83","external_id":"475f0dde-a4ec-4f91-b2b4-de36e7b1a6ca","vaultid":null,"uId":"1299b552-78d2-4b06-82b3-ad995d3460fa","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In hindsight, given the ideas about the natural world circulating among British scientists in the 1800s, the theory of natural selection was an obvious next step. It may not have been a coincidence, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> that Charles Darwin and Alfred Wallace arrived at the concept independently. Indeed, contrary to the popular myth of the lone genius, theirs is not the first paradigm-shifting theory to have emerged from multiple scholars working in parallel.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"125507ea-2aaa-46bf-9a16-d0f0a95debd0","content":"<p>however,</p>"},{"id":"8a170ec2-1a89-4848-b325-cb531038e4ce","content":"<p>then,</p>"},{"id":"34184b1e-95cd-45c2-82fe-4050e2a0c521","content":"<p>moreover,</p>"},{"id":"fae5e2f9-d60c-479e-8800-ebf89ae51ec7","content":"<p>for example,</p>"}],"keys":["8a170ec2-1a89-4848-b325-cb531038e4ce"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"Then\" signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about Darwin and Wallace follows logically from the previous information. In other words, both scientists independently arriving at the theory of natural selection was, arguably, an expected outcome of the circumstances mentioned in the previous sentence.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"however\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence contrasts with the previous information about the ideas circulating among British scientists in the 1800s. Instead, this claim follows logically from that information. Choice C is incorrect because \"moreover\" illogically signals that the claim in this sentence merely adds to the previous information about the ideas circulating among British scientists in the 1800s. Instead, this claim follows logically from that information. Choice D is incorrect because \"for example\" illogically signals that this sentence provides an example supporting the previous information about the ideas circulating among British scientists in the 1800s. Instead, it presents a claim that follows logically from that information.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323043,"updateDate":1730147323043}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323043, 1730147323043),
    ($SATQ$441f0505$SATQ$, $SATQ$e0ba20be-ade1-492d-8844-c299be07cc8d$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>A lever is a simple machine consisting of a rigid beam and a fulcrum.</li>
<li>The fulcrum is the point about which the beam pivots.</li>
<li>The input force (effort) is the force applied to the lever.</li>
<li>The output force (load) is the force that the lever exerts on another object.</li>
<li>In first-class levers, the fulcrum is located between the effort and the load.</li>
<li>In second-class levers, the load is located between the effort and the fulcrum.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to contrast first-class levers and second-class levers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"714cf316-9f28-4114-81b2-73221b939e0a","content":"<p>In levers, the effort is the force applied to the lever; the load, in contrast, is the force that the lever exerts on another object.</p>"},{"id":"0c41b3b4-aed1-4d3b-92d6-d4f0843368be","content":"<p>In first-class and second-class levers, the fulcrum and the load are in different locations.</p>"},{"id":"0542d1e0-54ae-4017-b9b2-f1649492a067","content":"<p>First-class levers are simple machines consisting of a rigid beam and a fulcrum, but then again, the same is true of second-class levers.</p>"},{"id":"a97078fc-b786-42bc-9296-afdb3097ca84","content":"<p>In first-class levers, the fulcrum is located between the effort and the load, but in second-class levers, the load is located between the effort and the fulcrum.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a97078fc-b786-42bc-9296-afdb3097ca84"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence contrasts first-class levers and second-class levers, explaining that the fulcrum in a first-class lever is between the effort and the load, whereas in a second-class lever the load is between the effort and the fulcrum.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence defines two terms associated with levers; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast first-class levers and second-class levers. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence seems to acknowledge a general difference in fulcrum and load locations between first-class and second-class levers, it does not specify what this difference is. Moreover, the sentence could be read as emphasizing a similarity&mdash;that in both types of levers, the fulcrum and load are in different locations. The sentence thus fails to effectively contrast the two types of levers. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes a similarity between first-class and second-class levers; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast them.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"441f0505","external_id":"e0ba20be-ade1-492d-8844-c299be07cc8d","vaultid":null,"uId":"1940c922-d9ac-4353-b4a1-b57356ed8a10","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A lever is a simple machine consisting of a rigid beam and a fulcrum.</li>\n<li>The fulcrum is the point about which the beam pivots.</li>\n<li>The input force (effort) is the force applied to the lever.</li>\n<li>The output force (load) is the force that the lever exerts on another object.</li>\n<li>In first-class levers, the fulcrum is located between the effort and the load.</li>\n<li>In second-class levers, the load is located between the effort and the fulcrum.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to contrast first-class levers and second-class levers. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"714cf316-9f28-4114-81b2-73221b939e0a","content":"<p>In levers, the effort is the force applied to the lever; the load, in contrast, is the force that the lever exerts on another object.</p>"},{"id":"0c41b3b4-aed1-4d3b-92d6-d4f0843368be","content":"<p>In first-class and second-class levers, the fulcrum and the load are in different locations.</p>"},{"id":"0542d1e0-54ae-4017-b9b2-f1649492a067","content":"<p>First-class levers are simple machines consisting of a rigid beam and a fulcrum, but then again, the same is true of second-class levers.</p>"},{"id":"a97078fc-b786-42bc-9296-afdb3097ca84","content":"<p>In first-class levers, the fulcrum is located between the effort and the load, but in second-class levers, the load is located between the effort and the fulcrum.</p>"}],"keys":["a97078fc-b786-42bc-9296-afdb3097ca84"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence contrasts first-class levers and second-class levers, explaining that the fulcrum in a first-class lever is between the effort and the load, whereas in a second-class lever the load is between the effort and the fulcrum.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence defines two terms associated with levers; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast first-class levers and second-class levers. Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence seems to acknowledge a general difference in fulcrum and load locations between first-class and second-class levers, it does not specify what this difference is. Moreover, the sentence could be read as emphasizing a similarity&mdash;that in both types of levers, the fulcrum and load are in different locations. The sentence thus fails to effectively contrast the two types of levers. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence describes a similarity between first-class and second-class levers; it doesn&rsquo;t contrast them.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323032,"updateDate":1730147323032}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323032, 1730147323032),
    ($SATQ$459df2ba$SATQ$, $SATQ$9ed92caf-8ee3-4252-b86b-47b5bce47519$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Modularity of mind is the notion that the mind is at least partly composed of innate neural structures (modules) that perform fast, necessary tasks.</li>
<li><span data-ssml-say-as="date">1983</span>: cognitive scientist Jerry <span data-ssml-sub-alias="A">A.</span> Fodor hypothesized that low-level cognitive systems (<span data-ssml-sub-alias="e g">e.g.</span>, perception, language) are modular.</li>
<li>In Fodorian modularity, high-level systems (<span data-ssml-sub-alias="e g">e.g.</span>, reasoning) are not modular.</li>
<li><span data-ssml-say-as="date">2003</span>: cognitive scientist Peter Carruthers proposed the massive modularity hypothesis (MMH).</li>
<li>MMH expands modularity to include all cognitive systems.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to compare Fodor&rsquo;s hypothesis with Carruthers&rsquo;s. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"a2f27e94-eebe-4544-85d3-38e66098d9e2","content":"<p>In considering some but not all cognitive systems modular, Fodorian modularity is not as expansive in its definition of modularity as MMH is.</p>"},{"id":"e5e55621-0ed4-4a96-b8eb-08a48cd8e3a1","content":"<p>Following Fodor&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1983</span> hypothesis, Carruthers proposed that modularity of mind includes all cognitive systems.</p>"},{"id":"0e34121c-e666-4b28-82d2-745bcfd45e6b","content":"<p>The hypotheses of Fodor and Carruthers differ in whether they consider low-level cognitive systems, such as perception and language, modular.</p>"},{"id":"b224ad71-49c1-42b1-b9aa-d3792cdb329f","content":"<p>In <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2003</span>, Carruthers proposed the massive modularity hypothesis, disagreeing with Fodor&rsquo;s earlier hypothesis that the mind is composed of innate neural structures.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["a2f27e94-eebe-4544-85d3-38e66098d9e2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence compares Fodor&rsquo;s hypothesis with Carruthers&rsquo;s, noting that because Fodorian modularity considers some but not all cognitive systems modular, it is not as expansive in its definition of modularity as Carruthers&rsquo;s MMH, which includes all cognitive systems.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes Carruthers&rsquo;s hypothesis&mdash;that modularity of mind includes all cognitive systems&mdash;but indicates only that this hypothesis followed Fodor&rsquo;s 1983 hypothesis rather than making a comparison between the hypotheses. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents a difference between Fodor&rsquo;s and Carruthers&rsquo;s hypotheses. According to the information in the notes, both Fodor and Carruthers consider low-level cognitive systems modular, and their difference lies in whether they also consider high-level systems modular. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents a difference between Fodor&rsquo;s and Carruthers&rsquo;s hypotheses. According to the information in the notes, both Fodor and Carruthers consider the mind to be at least partly composed of innate neural structures (modules). Their difference lies in whether they consider high-level systems modular.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"459df2ba","external_id":"9ed92caf-8ee3-4252-b86b-47b5bce47519","vaultid":null,"uId":"6972b04d-2112-4376-b8c5-a0d6182682c2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Modularity of mind is the notion that the mind is at least partly composed of innate neural structures (modules) that perform fast, necessary tasks.</li>\n<li><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1983</span>: cognitive scientist Jerry <span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"A\">A.</span> Fodor hypothesized that low-level cognitive systems (<span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"e g\">e.g.</span>, perception, language) are modular.</li>\n<li>In Fodorian modularity, high-level systems (<span data-ssml-sub-alias=\"e g\">e.g.</span>, reasoning) are not modular.</li>\n<li><span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2003</span>: cognitive scientist Peter Carruthers proposed the massive modularity hypothesis (MMH).</li>\n<li>MMH expands modularity to include all cognitive systems.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to compare Fodor&rsquo;s hypothesis with Carruthers&rsquo;s. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"a2f27e94-eebe-4544-85d3-38e66098d9e2","content":"<p>In considering some but not all cognitive systems modular, Fodorian modularity is not as expansive in its definition of modularity as MMH is.</p>"},{"id":"e5e55621-0ed4-4a96-b8eb-08a48cd8e3a1","content":"<p>Following Fodor&rsquo;s <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">1983</span> hypothesis, Carruthers proposed that modularity of mind includes all cognitive systems.</p>"},{"id":"0e34121c-e666-4b28-82d2-745bcfd45e6b","content":"<p>The hypotheses of Fodor and Carruthers differ in whether they consider low-level cognitive systems, such as perception and language, modular.</p>"},{"id":"b224ad71-49c1-42b1-b9aa-d3792cdb329f","content":"<p>In <span data-ssml-say-as=\"date\">2003</span>, Carruthers proposed the massive modularity hypothesis, disagreeing with Fodor&rsquo;s earlier hypothesis that the mind is composed of innate neural structures.</p>"}],"keys":["a2f27e94-eebe-4544-85d3-38e66098d9e2"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence compares Fodor&rsquo;s hypothesis with Carruthers&rsquo;s, noting that because Fodorian modularity considers some but not all cognitive systems modular, it is not as expansive in its definition of modularity as Carruthers&rsquo;s MMH, which includes all cognitive systems.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence describes Carruthers&rsquo;s hypothesis&mdash;that modularity of mind includes all cognitive systems&mdash;but indicates only that this hypothesis followed Fodor&rsquo;s 1983 hypothesis rather than making a comparison between the hypotheses. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents a difference between Fodor&rsquo;s and Carruthers&rsquo;s hypotheses. According to the information in the notes, both Fodor and Carruthers consider low-level cognitive systems modular, and their difference lies in whether they also consider high-level systems modular. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence misrepresents a difference between Fodor&rsquo;s and Carruthers&rsquo;s hypotheses. According to the information in the notes, both Fodor and Carruthers consider the mind to be at least partly composed of innate neural structures (modules). Their difference lies in whether they consider high-level systems modular.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1776362771169,"updateDate":1776362771169}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1776362771169, 1776362771169),
    ($SATQ$45eaf7fb$SATQ$, $SATQ$49d2ee0d-e9e6-4f09-8a43-65700c0eda3f$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Tecozautla is a municipality in the state of Hidalgo, Mexico.</li>
<li>Municipalities are governmental regions responsible for providing many public services to their residents.</li>
<li>One service they provide is street lighting.&nbsp;</li>
<li>Tecozautla covers an area of roughly 535 <span aria-hidden="true">km&sup2;</span><span class="sr-only">km squared</span>.</li>
<li>Hidalgo is divided into 84 municipalities.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize the size of Tecozautla. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d146f01b-e103-4011-837d-1d8f0131e873","content":"<p>The municipality of Tecozautla in Hidalgo, Mexico, covers an area of roughly 535 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">km&sup2;</span><span class=\"sr-only\">km squared</span>.</p>"},{"id":"c7f05776-1180-4e20-80b6-e5767612c1e6","content":"<p>Providing street lighting is just one example of the public services that municipalities provide.</p>"},{"id":"43586f0b-d39e-42c8-a31f-ff2ab3251db4","content":"<p>Tecozautla is one of 84 governmental regions, known as municipalities, across Hidalgo.</p>"},{"id":"441eba3a-c21f-4006-9ea7-f08c8734fc80","content":"<p>Tecozautla&mdash;a governmental region in the state of Hidalgo, Mexico&mdash;provides many public services to its residents.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d146f01b-e103-4011-837d-1d8f0131e873"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the size of Tecozautla, noting that it covers an area of roughly 535 <span aria-hidden="true">km&sup2;</span><span class="sr-only">km squared</span>.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence gives an example of a public service that municipalities provide; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the size of Tecozautla. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about Tecozautla, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize Tecozautla&rsquo;s size. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about Tecozautla, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize Tecozautla&rsquo;s size.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"45eaf7fb","external_id":"49d2ee0d-e9e6-4f09-8a43-65700c0eda3f","vaultid":null,"uId":"974981b9-ba89-4de4-9df3-a1ed993a3773","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Tecozautla is a municipality in the state of Hidalgo, Mexico.</li>\n<li>Municipalities are governmental regions responsible for providing many public services to their residents.</li>\n<li>One service they provide is street lighting.&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Tecozautla covers an area of roughly 535 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">km&sup2;</span><span class=\"sr-only\">km squared</span>.</li>\n<li>Hidalgo is divided into 84 municipalities.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize the size of Tecozautla. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d146f01b-e103-4011-837d-1d8f0131e873","content":"<p>The municipality of Tecozautla in Hidalgo, Mexico, covers an area of roughly 535 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">km&sup2;</span><span class=\"sr-only\">km squared</span>.</p>"},{"id":"c7f05776-1180-4e20-80b6-e5767612c1e6","content":"<p>Providing street lighting is just one example of the public services that municipalities provide.</p>"},{"id":"43586f0b-d39e-42c8-a31f-ff2ab3251db4","content":"<p>Tecozautla is one of 84 governmental regions, known as municipalities, across Hidalgo.</p>"},{"id":"441eba3a-c21f-4006-9ea7-f08c8734fc80","content":"<p>Tecozautla&mdash;a governmental region in the state of Hidalgo, Mexico&mdash;provides many public services to its residents.</p>"}],"keys":["d146f01b-e103-4011-837d-1d8f0131e873"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes the size of Tecozautla, noting that it covers an area of roughly 535 <span aria-hidden=\"true\">km&sup2;</span><span class=\"sr-only\">km squared</span>.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence gives an example of a public service that municipalities provide; it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize the size of Tecozautla. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about Tecozautla, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize Tecozautla&rsquo;s size. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence provides information about Tecozautla, it doesn&rsquo;t emphasize Tecozautla&rsquo;s size.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061177,"updateDate":1755117061177}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061177, 1755117061177),
    ($SATQ$4703eafb$SATQ$, $SATQ$9b3cf440-7790-49d1-a3c2-47a194d3c749$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Following the American Revolutionary War, North American foodways underwent a radical transformation, fueled in large part by spiking consumer demand for certain grains. The cultivation, trade, and transportation of maize and wheat, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> reconfigured the continent&rsquo;s existing regional foodways into a globally oriented food system.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9a8171e2-c702-42da-bb62-f72c79ea30c1","content":"<p>in particular,</p>"},{"id":"dcaac8f8-fe94-4a88-b347-320dcac42b03","content":"<p>alternatively,</p>"},{"id":"2be09e8d-2c67-491f-8329-65fe385051cb","content":"<p>by comparison,</p>"},{"id":"5f8a7e69-bc9b-44b4-b36c-635a42753df1","content":"<p>second of all,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["9a8171e2-c702-42da-bb62-f72c79ea30c1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. "In particular" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that maize and wheat supply chains transformed North American foodways into a global food system&mdash;provides specific, precise details elaborating on the more general information in the previous sentence about the transformation of North American foodways (with maize and wheat the "certain grains" at the center of it).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because "alternatively" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an alternative option to the previous information about the transformation of North American foodways. Instead, the roles of maize and wheat in creating a global food system are specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice C is incorrect because "by comparison" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is being compared to the previous information about the transformation of North American foodways. Instead, the roles of maize and wheat in creating a global food system are specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice D is incorrect because "second of all" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a second, separate claim from the previous claim that North American foodways were transformed. Instead, the roles of maize and wheat in creating a global food system are specific, precise details elaborating on that information, rather than a separate claim.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4703eafb","external_id":"9b3cf440-7790-49d1-a3c2-47a194d3c749","vaultid":null,"uId":"ec462570-10a1-4c5b-92b6-230c2592f788","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Following the American Revolutionary War, North American foodways underwent a radical transformation, fueled in large part by spiking consumer demand for certain grains. The cultivation, trade, and transportation of maize and wheat, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> reconfigured the continent&rsquo;s existing regional foodways into a globally oriented food system.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9a8171e2-c702-42da-bb62-f72c79ea30c1","content":"<p>in particular,</p>"},{"id":"dcaac8f8-fe94-4a88-b347-320dcac42b03","content":"<p>alternatively,</p>"},{"id":"2be09e8d-2c67-491f-8329-65fe385051cb","content":"<p>by comparison,</p>"},{"id":"5f8a7e69-bc9b-44b4-b36c-635a42753df1","content":"<p>second of all,</p>"}],"keys":["9a8171e2-c702-42da-bb62-f72c79ea30c1"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. \"In particular\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that maize and wheat supply chains transformed North American foodways into a global food system&mdash;provides specific, precise details elaborating on the more general information in the previous sentence about the transformation of North American foodways (with maize and wheat the \"certain grains\" at the center of it).</p><p>Choice B is incorrect because \"alternatively\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is an alternative option to the previous information about the transformation of North American foodways. Instead, the roles of maize and wheat in creating a global food system are specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice C is incorrect because \"by comparison\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is being compared to the previous information about the transformation of North American foodways. Instead, the roles of maize and wheat in creating a global food system are specific, precise details elaborating on that information. Choice D is incorrect because \"second of all\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a second, separate claim from the previous claim that North American foodways were transformed. Instead, the roles of maize and wheat in creating a global food system are specific, precise details elaborating on that information, rather than a separate claim.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669211,"updateDate":1743430669211}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669211, 1743430669211),
    ($SATQ$47547d07$SATQ$, $SATQ$646459cd-e5c2-497c-a3f1-a2dd38fd1867$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$b6ce0eb3-8019-464b-bedd-2d402e38d451$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In June, female loggerhead sea turtles will swim back to the sandy beaches where they were born to lay eggs of their own. First, the turtle will dig her nest in the sand. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> she will lay up to 100 eggs in the nest. Finally, she will cover it all with sand, before returning to the ocean.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"b980eba3-cc40-4f05-9bac-0e7674155386","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"d8435ad3-4e30-4c4d-b961-712e502e8a58","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"eb880d77-8c02-4540-98de-036a5fcafac2","content":"<p>Next,</p>"},{"id":"91562746-ad33-4798-8afd-e6bbb84877d4","content":"<p>For example,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["eb880d77-8c02-4540-98de-036a5fcafac2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Next&rdquo; logically signals that the egg laying in this sentence is the next step in the sequence of events described in the other sentences. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;by contrast&rdquo; illogically signals that the egg laying in this sentence contrasts with the nest digging in the previous sentence. Instead, the egg laying follows the nest digging as the next step in the sequence of events. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the egg laying in this sentence is similar to the nest digging in the previous sentence. Though the two actions are related, they are not similar. Instead, the egg laying follows the nest digging as the next step in the sequence of events. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the egg laying in this sentence is an example of the nest digging in the previous sentence. Instead, the egg laying follows the nest digging as the next step in the sequence of events. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-241-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$27da2d02-358c-4331-bf5c-06c3d668664a$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"47547d07","external_id":"646459cd-e5c2-497c-a3f1-a2dd38fd1867","vaultid":"b6ce0eb3-8019-464b-bedd-2d402e38d451","uId":"3e2e04fd-9e1f-4916-989e-706f4f058558","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In June, female loggerhead sea turtles will swim back to the sandy beaches where they were born to lay eggs of their own. First, the turtle will dig her nest in the sand. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> she will lay up to 100 eggs in the nest. Finally, she will cover it all with sand, before returning to the ocean.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"b980eba3-cc40-4f05-9bac-0e7674155386","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"d8435ad3-4e30-4c4d-b961-712e502e8a58","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"},{"id":"eb880d77-8c02-4540-98de-036a5fcafac2","content":"<p>Next,</p>"},{"id":"91562746-ad33-4798-8afd-e6bbb84877d4","content":"<p>For example,</p>"}],"keys":["eb880d77-8c02-4540-98de-036a5fcafac2"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. &ldquo;Next&rdquo; logically signals that the egg laying in this sentence is the next step in the sequence of events described in the other sentences. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;by contrast&rdquo; illogically signals that the egg laying in this sentence contrasts with the nest digging in the previous sentence. Instead, the egg laying follows the nest digging as the next step in the sequence of events. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;similarly&rdquo; illogically signals that the egg laying in this sentence is similar to the nest digging in the previous sentence. Though the two actions are related, they are not similar. Instead, the egg laying follows the nest digging as the next step in the sequence of events. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the egg laying in this sentence is an example of the nest digging in the previous sentence. Instead, the egg laying follows the nest digging as the next step in the sequence of events. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-241-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"27da2d02-358c-4331-bf5c-06c3d668664a","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959333,"updateDate":1691007959333}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959333, 1691007959333),
    ($SATQ$47e238be$SATQ$, $SATQ$a2f6277c-a89c-4a0f-b14a-5e4ddc3e2148$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$2a0f0a94-ad23-477f-af3f-b41fbf014ade$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 7, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Seismologists Kaiqing Yuan and Barbara Romanowicz have proposed that the magma fueling Iceland&rsquo;s more than 30 active volcano systems emerges from deep within Earth. The great depths involved&mdash;nearly 3,000 km&mdash;mark Iceland&rsquo;s volcanoes as extreme outliers; <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> many of Earth&rsquo;s volcanoes are fed by shallow pockets of magma found less than 15 km below the surface.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cd4fcd76-305e-4a37-8cb6-1ddbb4ad074f","content":"<p>indeed,</p>"},{"id":"1e241a09-aecd-4d7b-804e-d460cf32b958","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"79d13023-d9d8-44a8-ac21-4bef91714ac4","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"},{"id":"dee0922b-2c4e-4dca-bb51-0c0cdba34d31","content":"<p>consequently,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["cd4fcd76-305e-4a37-8cb6-1ddbb4ad074f"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The second part of the sentence says that many volcanoes use shallow pockets of magma. This is an elaboration of the same underlying idea from the first part of the sentence, which says that the super deep magma of Icelandic volcanoes&rsquo; makes them outliers. &ldquo;Indeed&rdquo; is a transition used for elaborating on the same idea, so it fits the context perfectly. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But these two parts of the sentence agree with each other, so &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t make sense. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates the addition of a new idea. But the second part of the sentence isn&rsquo;t adding a new idea: it&rsquo;s elaborating on the same idea expressed in the first part of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a cause-and-effect transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The fact that Iceland&rsquo;s deep-magma volcanoes are outliers doesn&rsquo;t cause many other volcanoes to get fed by shallow pockets of magma. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-KA-TRA-H004$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$14fbec80-c40d-48c3-a5e2-5a298375919d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"47e238be","external_id":"a2f6277c-a89c-4a0f-b14a-5e4ddc3e2148","vaultid":"2a0f0a94-ad23-477f-af3f-b41fbf014ade","uId":"98a2b5dc-e4ed-45c4-ac5b-9338664fed19","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":7,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Seismologists Kaiqing Yuan and Barbara Romanowicz have proposed that the magma fueling Iceland&rsquo;s more than 30 active volcano systems emerges from deep within Earth. The great depths involved&mdash;nearly 3,000 km&mdash;mark Iceland&rsquo;s volcanoes as extreme outliers; <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> many of Earth&rsquo;s volcanoes are fed by shallow pockets of magma found less than 15 km below the surface.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cd4fcd76-305e-4a37-8cb6-1ddbb4ad074f","content":"<p>indeed,</p>"},{"id":"1e241a09-aecd-4d7b-804e-d460cf32b958","content":"<p>nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"79d13023-d9d8-44a8-ac21-4bef91714ac4","content":"<p>in addition,</p>"},{"id":"dee0922b-2c4e-4dca-bb51-0c0cdba34d31","content":"<p>consequently,</p>"}],"keys":["cd4fcd76-305e-4a37-8cb6-1ddbb4ad074f"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The second part of the sentence says that many volcanoes use shallow pockets of magma. This is an elaboration of the same underlying idea from the first part of the sentence, which says that the super deep magma of Icelandic volcanoes&rsquo; makes them outliers. &ldquo;Indeed&rdquo; is a transition used for elaborating on the same idea, so it fits the context perfectly. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. This choice uses a disagreement transition. But these two parts of the sentence agree with each other, so &ldquo;nevertheless&rdquo; doesn&rsquo;t make sense. Choice C is incorrect. This choice uses a transition that indicates the addition of a new idea. But the second part of the sentence isn&rsquo;t adding a new idea: it&rsquo;s elaborating on the same idea expressed in the first part of the sentence. Choice D is incorrect. This choice uses a cause-and-effect transition, which doesn&rsquo;t make sense here. The fact that Iceland&rsquo;s deep-magma volcanoes are outliers doesn&rsquo;t cause many other volcanoes to get fed by shallow pockets of magma. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-KA-TRA-H004","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"14fbec80-c40d-48c3-a5e2-5a298375919d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959373,"updateDate":1691007959373}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959373, 1691007959373),
    ($SATQ$480ade7e$SATQ$, $SATQ$9e08e07b-a735-4642-a734-819ff9d186dc$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$50345b7c-aab4-4ff4-8b15-6df211c23e1b$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In response to adverse environmental conditions, many plants produce abscisic acid (ABA), a stress hormone. ABA triggers a slowdown in the biological processes of most plants. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> when the mustard plant <em>Schrenkiella parvula</em> produces ABA in response to an environmental stressor, the hormone triggers accelerated growth. &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"c0fca6da-f3c7-4cee-bcc9-24c4528b767c","content":"<p>Moreover,</p>"},{"id":"84f24d95-b852-4b70-9dfe-b5fc7a60bb4c","content":"<p>In contrast,</p>"},{"id":"73908843-6f24-4d65-b5ed-bfdf779dd007","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"8df5b835-0163-4c96-9a0c-1946f7fa2920","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["84f24d95-b852-4b70-9dfe-b5fc7a60bb4c"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;In contrast&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that ABA triggers accelerated growth in the mustard plant <em>Schrenkiella parvula</em>&mdash;contrasts with the previous information about ABA triggering a slowdown in most plants&rsquo; biological processes. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;moreover&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence about the mustard plant merely adds to the previous information about the effects of ABA. Instead, it contrasts with that information.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence about the mustard plant provides an example consistent with the previous information about the effects of ABA. Instead, it contrasts with that information. &nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;thus&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence about the mustard plant is a consequence, or result, of the previous information about the effects of ABA. Instead, it contrasts with that information.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-273-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$041c07ce-f1a2-4c44-a284-c5d475e9c6fb$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"480ade7e","external_id":"9e08e07b-a735-4642-a734-819ff9d186dc","vaultid":"50345b7c-aab4-4ff4-8b15-6df211c23e1b","uId":"c623e14c-27f2-4ba9-82a5-c695addda02a","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In response to adverse environmental conditions, many plants produce abscisic acid (ABA), a stress hormone. ABA triggers a slowdown in the biological processes of most plants. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> when the mustard plant <em>Schrenkiella parvula</em> produces ABA in response to an environmental stressor, the hormone triggers accelerated growth. &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"c0fca6da-f3c7-4cee-bcc9-24c4528b767c","content":"<p>Moreover,</p>"},{"id":"84f24d95-b852-4b70-9dfe-b5fc7a60bb4c","content":"<p>In contrast,</p>"},{"id":"73908843-6f24-4d65-b5ed-bfdf779dd007","content":"<p>For example,</p>"},{"id":"8df5b835-0163-4c96-9a0c-1946f7fa2920","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"}],"keys":["84f24d95-b852-4b70-9dfe-b5fc7a60bb4c"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;In contrast&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that ABA triggers accelerated growth in the mustard plant <em>Schrenkiella parvula</em>&mdash;contrasts with the previous information about ABA triggering a slowdown in most plants&rsquo; biological processes. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;moreover&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence about the mustard plant merely adds to the previous information about the effects of ABA. Instead, it contrasts with that information.&nbsp;Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;for example&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence about the mustard plant provides an example consistent with the previous information about the effects of ABA. Instead, it contrasts with that information. &nbsp;Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;thus&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence about the mustard plant is a consequence, or result, of the previous information about the effects of ABA. Instead, it contrasts with that information.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-273-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"041c07ce-f1a2-4c44-a284-c5d475e9c6fb","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959334,"updateDate":1691007959334}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959334, 1691007959334),
    ($SATQ$48d0bb34$SATQ$, $SATQ$d2215e4a-5d7e-4bd8-84b4-b4892566abd9$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$82761d75-5847-46bd-9626-21868477d748$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Sam Maloof (1916&ndash;2009) was an American woodworker and furniture designer.</li>
<li>He was the son of Lebanese immigrants.</li>
<li>He received a &ldquo;genius grant&rdquo; from the John D. and Catherine T. MacArthur Foundation in 1985.</li>
<li>The Museum of Fine Arts in Boston, Massachusetts, owns a rocking chair that Maloof made from walnut wood.</li>
<li>The armrests and the seat of the chair are sleek and contoured, and the back consists of seven spindle-like slats.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to describe the rocking chair to an audience unfamiliar with Sam Maloof. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d8c2296a-d8e0-44ba-80dd-5c361f8f72ec","content":"<p>With its sleek, contoured armrests and seat, the walnut rocking chair in Boston&rsquo;s Museum of Fine Arts is just one piece of furniture created by American woodworker Sam Maloof.</p>"},{"id":"ce5dca88-7722-425a-ad97-5bd337be0d74","content":"<p>Sam Maloof was born in 1916 and died in 2009, and during his life, he made a chair that you can see if you visit the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston.</p>"},{"id":"bffa896f-c99c-4cd6-a49f-77cb75a330ee","content":"<p>Furniture designer Sam Maloof was a recipient of one of the John D. and Catherine T. MacArthur Foundation&rsquo;s &ldquo;genius grants.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"b28cf78d-4e59-4a46-abfe-8df01e535f87","content":"<p>The rocking chair is made from walnut, and it has been shaped such that its armrests and seat are sleek and contoured.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["d8c2296a-d8e0-44ba-80dd-5c361f8f72ec"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence effectively describes the rocking chair to an audience unfamiliar with Sam Maloof, noting its sleek, contoured armrests and seat and explaining that Sam Maloof (the walnut chair&rsquo;s creator) was an American woodworker. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence explains who Sam Maloof was and mentions a chair, it doesn&rsquo;t describe the chair. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence explains who Sam Maloof was, it doesn&rsquo;t describe the rocking chair. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence describes the rocking chair, it doesn&rsquo;t explain who Sam Maloof was. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-706-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$bf5fc9d0-f84a-4e3e-8a78-b9c7cc4841e4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"48d0bb34","external_id":"d2215e4a-5d7e-4bd8-84b4-b4892566abd9","vaultid":"82761d75-5847-46bd-9626-21868477d748","uId":"08de632d-5d4a-4fe8-87b5-d3f7cea73481","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Sam Maloof (1916&ndash;2009) was an American woodworker and furniture designer.</li>\n<li>He was the son of Lebanese immigrants.</li>\n<li>He received a &ldquo;genius grant&rdquo; from the John D. and Catherine T. MacArthur Foundation in 1985.</li>\n<li>The Museum of Fine Arts in Boston, Massachusetts, owns a rocking chair that Maloof made from walnut wood.</li>\n<li>The armrests and the seat of the chair are sleek and contoured, and the back consists of seven spindle-like slats.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to describe the rocking chair to an audience unfamiliar with Sam Maloof. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d8c2296a-d8e0-44ba-80dd-5c361f8f72ec","content":"<p>With its sleek, contoured armrests and seat, the walnut rocking chair in Boston&rsquo;s Museum of Fine Arts is just one piece of furniture created by American woodworker Sam Maloof.</p>"},{"id":"ce5dca88-7722-425a-ad97-5bd337be0d74","content":"<p>Sam Maloof was born in 1916 and died in 2009, and during his life, he made a chair that you can see if you visit the Museum of Fine Arts in Boston.</p>"},{"id":"bffa896f-c99c-4cd6-a49f-77cb75a330ee","content":"<p>Furniture designer Sam Maloof was a recipient of one of the John D. and Catherine T. MacArthur Foundation&rsquo;s &ldquo;genius grants.&rdquo;</p>"},{"id":"b28cf78d-4e59-4a46-abfe-8df01e535f87","content":"<p>The rocking chair is made from walnut, and it has been shaped such that its armrests and seat are sleek and contoured.</p>"}],"keys":["d8c2296a-d8e0-44ba-80dd-5c361f8f72ec"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence effectively describes the rocking chair to an audience unfamiliar with Sam Maloof, noting its sleek, contoured armrests and seat and explaining that Sam Maloof (the walnut chair&rsquo;s creator) was an American woodworker. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While the sentence explains who Sam Maloof was and mentions a chair, it doesn&rsquo;t describe the chair. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence explains who Sam Maloof was, it doesn&rsquo;t describe the rocking chair. Choice D is incorrect. While the sentence describes the rocking chair, it doesn&rsquo;t explain who Sam Maloof was. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-706-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"bf5fc9d0-f84a-4e3e-8a78-b9c7cc4841e4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959347,"updateDate":1691007959347}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959347, 1691007959347),
    ($SATQ$49ecf985$SATQ$, $SATQ$cda0efee-82ff-4a4b-9bf9-5497adb01999$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>On March 3, 1991, Switzerland&rsquo;s government lowered the minimum voting age for its citizens from 20 to 18 years old. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> many people in Switzerland gained a new opportunity to participate in their country&rsquo;s political process.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"fa39f70b-c33d-41dd-acac-ab1e2ef235ca","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"6afdf3e5-10ea-4922-bb35-f8909584f7de","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"c6f571be-74d1-43e2-93c5-01f4be659e5f","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"5411ce28-f816-4661-9b8a-a1e44ee6353a","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6afdf3e5-10ea-4922-bb35-f8909584f7de"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. "As a result" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that many people in Switzerland gained a new opportunity to participate in their country&rsquo;s political process&mdash;is a result or consequence of the previous information about the Swiss government lowering the voting age.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "nevertheless" illogically signals that the information that follows is despite the information in the previous sentence about the Swiss government lowering the voting age. Instead, people gained a new opportunity to participate in Switzerland&rsquo;s political process as a result or consequence of that change. Choice C is incorrect because "by contrast" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence about the Swiss government lowering the voting age. Instead, people gained a new opportunity to participate in Switzerland&rsquo;s political process as a result or consequence of that change. Choice D is incorrect because "similarly" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the information about the Swiss government lowering the voting age in the previous sentence. Instead, people gained a new opportunity to participate in Switzerland&rsquo;s political process as a result or consequence of that change.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"49ecf985","external_id":"cda0efee-82ff-4a4b-9bf9-5497adb01999","vaultid":null,"uId":"623da47d-a91a-44d8-8dc8-64fbfeccf58b","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":1,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>On March 3, 1991, Switzerland&rsquo;s government lowered the minimum voting age for its citizens from 20 to 18 years old. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> many people in Switzerland gained a new opportunity to participate in their country&rsquo;s political process.&nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"fa39f70b-c33d-41dd-acac-ab1e2ef235ca","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"},{"id":"6afdf3e5-10ea-4922-bb35-f8909584f7de","content":"<p>As a result,</p>"},{"id":"c6f571be-74d1-43e2-93c5-01f4be659e5f","content":"<p>By contrast,</p>"},{"id":"5411ce28-f816-4661-9b8a-a1e44ee6353a","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}],"keys":["6afdf3e5-10ea-4922-bb35-f8909584f7de"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. \"As a result\" logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that many people in Switzerland gained a new opportunity to participate in their country&rsquo;s political process&mdash;is a result or consequence of the previous information about the Swiss government lowering the voting age.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"nevertheless\" illogically signals that the information that follows is despite the information in the previous sentence about the Swiss government lowering the voting age. Instead, people gained a new opportunity to participate in Switzerland&rsquo;s political process as a result or consequence of that change. Choice C is incorrect because \"by contrast\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the information in the previous sentence about the Swiss government lowering the voting age. Instead, people gained a new opportunity to participate in Switzerland&rsquo;s political process as a result or consequence of that change. Choice D is incorrect because \"similarly\" illogically signals that the information in this sentence is similar to the information about the Swiss government lowering the voting age in the previous sentence. Instead, people gained a new opportunity to participate in Switzerland&rsquo;s political process as a result or consequence of that change.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061192,"updateDate":1755117061192}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061192, 1755117061192),
    ($SATQ$49fe306b$SATQ$, $SATQ$dff9514d-6b0c-4b46-b344-9473f9e8f23d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$17cd206c-47df-469b-a7b2-1c69b05880b7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>From Earth, all the meteors in a meteor shower appear to originate from a single spot in the sky.</li>
<li>This spot is called the meteor shower&rsquo;s radiant.</li>
<li>The Perseid meteor shower is visible in the northern hemisphere in July and August.</li>
<li>Like many meteor showers, it is named for the location of its radiant.</li>
<li>Its radiant is located within the constellation Perseus.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to explain the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0c5df48f-5da4-420e-b950-ce0833f9fad5","content":"<p>The Perseid meteor shower is named for the constellation Perseus, the location of the meteor shower&rsquo;s radiant.</p>"},{"id":"1127fa50-d458-4ef9-a8ee-7e3b5487eaa0","content":"<p>A meteor shower&rsquo;s name may be linked to a single spot in the sky.</p>"},{"id":"786b0b7b-ff9c-4879-add2-472f625a9e13","content":"<p>The Perseid meteor shower, which has a radiant, is visible in the northern hemisphere in July and August.</p>"},{"id":"855ba88b-3ab5-406d-acb3-745500398bb4","content":"<p>From Earth, all the meteors in a meteor shower appear to originate from a radiant, such as the one within Perseus.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["0c5df48f-5da4-420e-b950-ce0833f9fad5"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence explains the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name: the constellation Perseus, where the meteor shower&rsquo;s radiant is located. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence makes a claim about meteor shower names in general; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name specifically. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates when and where the Perseid meteor shower is visible; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the origin of the meteor shower&rsquo;s name. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence discusses meteor showers in general; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-730-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$35df5671-6cac-49ea-90f3-b43cab23391d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"49fe306b","external_id":"dff9514d-6b0c-4b46-b344-9473f9e8f23d","vaultid":"17cd206c-47df-469b-a7b2-1c69b05880b7","uId":"81c0b9b6-a1cb-48c7-ac95-7ec2f3b3dd32","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>From Earth, all the meteors in a meteor shower appear to originate from a single spot in the sky.</li>\n<li>This spot is called the meteor shower&rsquo;s radiant.</li>\n<li>The Perseid meteor shower is visible in the northern hemisphere in July and August.</li>\n<li>Like many meteor showers, it is named for the location of its radiant.</li>\n<li>Its radiant is located within the constellation Perseus.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0c5df48f-5da4-420e-b950-ce0833f9fad5","content":"<p>The Perseid meteor shower is named for the constellation Perseus, the location of the meteor shower&rsquo;s radiant.</p>"},{"id":"1127fa50-d458-4ef9-a8ee-7e3b5487eaa0","content":"<p>A meteor shower&rsquo;s name may be linked to a single spot in the sky.</p>"},{"id":"786b0b7b-ff9c-4879-add2-472f625a9e13","content":"<p>The Perseid meteor shower, which has a radiant, is visible in the northern hemisphere in July and August.</p>"},{"id":"855ba88b-3ab5-406d-acb3-745500398bb4","content":"<p>From Earth, all the meteors in a meteor shower appear to originate from a radiant, such as the one within Perseus.</p>"}],"keys":["0c5df48f-5da4-420e-b950-ce0833f9fad5"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer. The sentence explains the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name: the constellation Perseus, where the meteor shower&rsquo;s radiant is located. </p><p>Choice B is incorrect. The sentence makes a claim about meteor shower names in general; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name specifically. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence indicates when and where the Perseid meteor shower is visible; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the origin of the meteor shower&rsquo;s name. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence discusses meteor showers in general; it doesn&rsquo;t explain the origin of the Perseid meteor shower&rsquo;s name. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-730-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"35df5671-6cac-49ea-90f3-b43cab23391d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$4b376902$SATQ$, $SATQ$cc20c403-bd6f-4a4e-8475-59c84ebaceb8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$836c799e-553d-4a68-9ada-52869c454129$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>NASA uses rovers, large remote vehicles with wheels, to explore the surface of Mars.</li>
<li>NASA&rsquo;s rovers can&rsquo;t explore regions inaccessible to wheeled vehicles.</li>
<li>Rovers are also heavy, making them difficult to land on the planet&rsquo;s surface.</li>
<li>Microprobes, robotic probes that weigh as little as 50 milligrams, could be deployed virtually anywhere on the surface of Mars.</li>
<li>Microprobes have been proposed as an alternative to rovers.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of microprobes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1f7ae7c1-9b8f-4cb2-a65f-1a0b21b25634","content":"<p>Despite being heavy, NASA&rsquo;s rovers can land successfully on the surface of Mars.</p>"},{"id":"adb51023-f99e-4490-8017-2ae2109a83f1","content":"<p>Microprobes, which weigh as little as 50 milligrams, could explore areas of Mars that are inaccessible to NASA&rsquo;s heavy, wheeled rovers.</p>"},{"id":"080e7183-36a7-4c2f-a5d4-a785e32269a0","content":"<p>NASA currently uses its rovers on Mars, but microprobes have been proposed as an alternative.</p>"},{"id":"433850e1-fd89-4227-8177-88b72886f913","content":"<p>Though they are different sizes, both microprobes and rovers can be used to explore the surface of Mars.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["adb51023-f99e-4490-8017-2ae2109a83f1"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence explains an advantage of microprobes, noting that because microprobes weigh as little as 50 milligrams, they can explore areas inaccessible to rovers. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that rovers can land successfully on Mars despite their weight; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of microprobes. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that microprobes have been proposed as an alternative to rovers, it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of microprobes. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between microprobes and rovers; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of microprobes. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-746-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$fd99505d-f992-4c9e-8bae-7b684cb739ba$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4b376902","external_id":"cc20c403-bd6f-4a4e-8475-59c84ebaceb8","vaultid":"836c799e-553d-4a68-9ada-52869c454129","uId":"7cfe32aa-bf9b-4844-bdf0-039061eb3e35","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>NASA uses rovers, large remote vehicles with wheels, to explore the surface of Mars.</li>\n<li>NASA&rsquo;s rovers can&rsquo;t explore regions inaccessible to wheeled vehicles.</li>\n<li>Rovers are also heavy, making them difficult to land on the planet&rsquo;s surface.</li>\n<li>Microprobes, robotic probes that weigh as little as 50 milligrams, could be deployed virtually anywhere on the surface of Mars.</li>\n<li>Microprobes have been proposed as an alternative to rovers.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of microprobes. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1f7ae7c1-9b8f-4cb2-a65f-1a0b21b25634","content":"<p>Despite being heavy, NASA&rsquo;s rovers can land successfully on the surface of Mars.</p>"},{"id":"adb51023-f99e-4490-8017-2ae2109a83f1","content":"<p>Microprobes, which weigh as little as 50 milligrams, could explore areas of Mars that are inaccessible to NASA&rsquo;s heavy, wheeled rovers.</p>"},{"id":"080e7183-36a7-4c2f-a5d4-a785e32269a0","content":"<p>NASA currently uses its rovers on Mars, but microprobes have been proposed as an alternative.</p>"},{"id":"433850e1-fd89-4227-8177-88b72886f913","content":"<p>Though they are different sizes, both microprobes and rovers can be used to explore the surface of Mars.</p>"}],"keys":["adb51023-f99e-4490-8017-2ae2109a83f1"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence explains an advantage of microprobes, noting that because microprobes weigh as little as 50 milligrams, they can explore areas inaccessible to rovers. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that rovers can land successfully on Mars despite their weight; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of microprobes. Choice C is incorrect. While the sentence mentions that microprobes have been proposed as an alternative to rovers, it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of microprobes. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between microprobes and rovers; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of microprobes. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-746-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"fd99505d-f992-4c9e-8bae-7b684cb739ba","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959350,"updateDate":1691007959350}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959350, 1691007959350),
    ($SATQ$4b7a84b0$SATQ$, $SATQ$58f0d5dd-524c-4f61-aa40-f10f19bbc0c2$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Mountain climbing routes that incorporate metal rungs and cables are known as via ferratas, from the Italian phrase for &ldquo;iron path.&rdquo; As climbing these routes has shifted from a mode of travel to a sporting activity, modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> new routes favor recreation over utility, aiming to provide a challenging climb or showcase dramatic scenery.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4cdf3d37-74f8-474b-8587-154718257fc9","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"098b6225-d296-42f3-9026-edac4c729da1","content":"<p>On the other hand,</p>"},{"id":"eedb6881-822f-46a2-a39a-77507898a947","content":"<p>More often,</p>"},{"id":"12efaabc-ce8d-4def-9236-278862d3b660","content":"<p>Nonetheless,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["eedb6881-822f-46a2-a39a-77507898a947"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. "More often" logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that new via ferratas favor recreation over utility&mdash;explains a difference between the new "sporting activity" routes and the older "mode of travel" routes. In so doing, it emphasizes and reinforces the previous claim ("modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because "additionally" illogically signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about new via ferratas adds a new, separate point to the previous claim ("modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit"). Instead, the second claim&mdash;that new routes favor recreation over utility&mdash;emphasizes and reinforces the previous one. Choice B is incorrect because "on the other hand" illogically signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about new via ferratas contrasts with or opposes the previous claim ("modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit").  Instead, the second claim&mdash;that new routes favor recreation over utility&mdash;emphasizes and reinforces the previous one. Choice D is incorrect because "nonetheless" illogically signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about new via ferratas is true despite the previous claim ("modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit"). Instead, the second claim&mdash;that new routes favor recreation over utility&mdash;emphasizes and reinforces the previous one.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4b7a84b0","external_id":"58f0d5dd-524c-4f61-aa40-f10f19bbc0c2","vaultid":null,"uId":"463f5b44-dd1a-4623-9d18-9881fc5ac906","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Mountain climbing routes that incorporate metal rungs and cables are known as via ferratas, from the Italian phrase for &ldquo;iron path.&rdquo; As climbing these routes has shifted from a mode of travel to a sporting activity, modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> new routes favor recreation over utility, aiming to provide a challenging climb or showcase dramatic scenery.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4cdf3d37-74f8-474b-8587-154718257fc9","content":"<p>Additionally,</p>"},{"id":"098b6225-d296-42f3-9026-edac4c729da1","content":"<p>On the other hand,</p>"},{"id":"eedb6881-822f-46a2-a39a-77507898a947","content":"<p>More often,</p>"},{"id":"12efaabc-ce8d-4def-9236-278862d3b660","content":"<p>Nonetheless,</p>"}],"keys":["eedb6881-822f-46a2-a39a-77507898a947"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. \"More often\" logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that new via ferratas favor recreation over utility&mdash;explains a difference between the new \"sporting activity\" routes and the older \"mode of travel\" routes. In so doing, it emphasizes and reinforces the previous claim (\"modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit\").</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because \"additionally\" illogically signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about new via ferratas adds a new, separate point to the previous claim (\"modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit\"). Instead, the second claim&mdash;that new routes favor recreation over utility&mdash;emphasizes and reinforces the previous one. Choice B is incorrect because \"on the other hand\" illogically signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about new via ferratas contrasts with or opposes the previous claim (\"modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit\").  Instead, the second claim&mdash;that new routes favor recreation over utility&mdash;emphasizes and reinforces the previous one. Choice D is incorrect because \"nonetheless\" illogically signals that this sentence&rsquo;s claim about new via ferratas is true despite the previous claim (\"modern via ferratas are rarely designed to simply reach a summit\"). Instead, the second claim&mdash;that new routes favor recreation over utility&mdash;emphasizes and reinforces the previous one.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669207,"updateDate":1743430669207}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669207, 1743430669207),
    ($SATQ$4b99b481$SATQ$, $SATQ$8167dacd-c02c-4102-911a-715929875ab3$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Scientists have developed a &ldquo;freeze-thaw&rdquo; battery that can retain 92% of its charge after twelve weeks.</li>
<li>The battery contains molten salt (a type of salt that liquifies when heated and solidifies at room temperature).</li>
<li>When the salt is in a liquid state, energy flows through the battery.</li>
<li>When the salt is in a solid state, energy stops flowing and is stored in the battery.</li>
<li>The stored (frozen) energy can be used by reheating (thawing) the battery.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to specify how the salt enables energy storage. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"0f437770-8e41-483e-853e-af0ef251210c","content":"<p>Scientists have developed a freeze-thaw battery that contains molten salt, which liquifies when heated and solidifies at room temperature.</p>"},{"id":"4edf5ec8-5816-4390-bbcf-c9c000b0ae84","content":"<p>The stored energy in a freeze-thaw battery, which contains molten salt, can be used by reheating the battery.</p>"},{"id":"78aee443-d634-4291-bc7a-e10776ac2bca","content":"<p>When the molten salt in a freeze-thaw battery solidifies at room temperature, energy stops flowing and can be stored in the battery.</p>"},{"id":"29167362-1a09-4851-a140-dba706e8f910","content":"<p>Molten salt allows a freeze-thaw battery to retain 92% of its charge after twelve weeks.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["78aee443-d634-4291-bc7a-e10776ac2bca"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence specifies how the salt in a freeze-thaw battery enables energy storage, explaining that energy stops flowing and can be stored when the salt solidifies at room temperature.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence explains some properties of molten salt; it doesn&rsquo;t specify how that salt enables energy storage. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates how the energy in a freeze-thaw battery can be released; it doesn&rsquo;t specify how the salt in the battery enables energy storage. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence specifies how much charge the freeze-thaw battery retains when storing energy; it doesn&rsquo;t specify how the salt in the battery enables energy storage.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4b99b481","external_id":"8167dacd-c02c-4102-911a-715929875ab3","vaultid":null,"uId":"1f4f6890-df86-44cd-a378-b26d9eafaed4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Scientists have developed a &ldquo;freeze-thaw&rdquo; battery that can retain 92% of its charge after twelve weeks.</li>\n<li>The battery contains molten salt (a type of salt that liquifies when heated and solidifies at room temperature).</li>\n<li>When the salt is in a liquid state, energy flows through the battery.</li>\n<li>When the salt is in a solid state, energy stops flowing and is stored in the battery.</li>\n<li>The stored (frozen) energy can be used by reheating (thawing) the battery.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to specify how the salt enables energy storage. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"0f437770-8e41-483e-853e-af0ef251210c","content":"<p>Scientists have developed a freeze-thaw battery that contains molten salt, which liquifies when heated and solidifies at room temperature.</p>"},{"id":"4edf5ec8-5816-4390-bbcf-c9c000b0ae84","content":"<p>The stored energy in a freeze-thaw battery, which contains molten salt, can be used by reheating the battery.</p>"},{"id":"78aee443-d634-4291-bc7a-e10776ac2bca","content":"<p>When the molten salt in a freeze-thaw battery solidifies at room temperature, energy stops flowing and can be stored in the battery.</p>"},{"id":"29167362-1a09-4851-a140-dba706e8f910","content":"<p>Molten salt allows a freeze-thaw battery to retain 92% of its charge after twelve weeks.</p>"}],"keys":["78aee443-d634-4291-bc7a-e10776ac2bca"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence specifies how the salt in a freeze-thaw battery enables energy storage, explaining that energy stops flowing and can be stored when the salt solidifies at room temperature.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence explains some properties of molten salt; it doesn&rsquo;t specify how that salt enables energy storage. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence indicates how the energy in a freeze-thaw battery can be released; it doesn&rsquo;t specify how the salt in the battery enables energy storage. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence specifies how much charge the freeze-thaw battery retains when storing energy; it doesn&rsquo;t specify how the salt in the battery enables energy storage.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323034,"updateDate":1730147323034}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323034, 1730147323034),
    ($SATQ$4c26f18a$SATQ$, $SATQ$28e393fa-f979-424b-b2f8-60c09c158037$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>By interlocking their bodies, ants can form bridges to help fellow ants cross gaps.</li>
<li>In 2020, Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin was inspired by ant behavior to design collaborative quadruped robots.&nbsp;</li>
<li>Over the course of a year, she designed, built, tested, and refined her robots.</li>
<li>Each robot is programmed to send a signal to another robot upon encountering a gap in a path.</li>
<li>The signaled robot connects to the back of the signaler robot via magnetic sensors and pushes it across the gap.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to begin a narrative about the creation of the robots. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d931837b-cf31-4c1c-9ca2-dd44c2487b9c","content":"<p>When one of Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin&rsquo;s robots encounters a gap in its path, it sends a signal to another robot; the signaled robot connects to the back of the signaler and pushes it across the gap.</p>"},{"id":"036978b6-6908-4735-8ef0-eadc8f179bd3","content":"<p>After a year, Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin had designed, built, tested, and refined her robots.</p>"},{"id":"4899f845-7590-4dd1-be81-db87845941e7","content":"<p>Inspired by ants, which form bridges with their interlocked bodies to help fellow ants cross gaps, Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin set out to design quadruped robots capable of similarly collaborative behavior.</p>"},{"id":"cd915ebe-438d-493e-bc6f-267aa553e320","content":"<p>Ants, which have inspired the design of robots, form bridges by interlocking their bodies.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["4899f845-7590-4dd1-be81-db87845941e7"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively begins a narrative about the creation of the robots, explaining that ants&rsquo; gap-crossing technique inspired Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin to "set out to design" robots that could collaborate in a similar manner.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes how the robots work together to cross gaps; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively begin a narrative about the creation of the robots. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence provides an overview of the steps Ozkan-Aydin took in creating the robots; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively begin a narrative about their creation. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains how ants form bridges; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively begin a narrative about the creation of the robots.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4c26f18a","external_id":"28e393fa-f979-424b-b2f8-60c09c158037","vaultid":null,"uId":"99726fc4-cd04-4a4d-bb8a-bbf6615b65ad","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>By interlocking their bodies, ants can form bridges to help fellow ants cross gaps.</li>\n<li>In 2020, Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin was inspired by ant behavior to design collaborative quadruped robots.&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Over the course of a year, she designed, built, tested, and refined her robots.</li>\n<li>Each robot is programmed to send a signal to another robot upon encountering a gap in a path.</li>\n<li>The signaled robot connects to the back of the signaler robot via magnetic sensors and pushes it across the gap.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to begin a narrative about the creation of the robots. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d931837b-cf31-4c1c-9ca2-dd44c2487b9c","content":"<p>When one of Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin&rsquo;s robots encounters a gap in its path, it sends a signal to another robot; the signaled robot connects to the back of the signaler and pushes it across the gap.</p>"},{"id":"036978b6-6908-4735-8ef0-eadc8f179bd3","content":"<p>After a year, Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin had designed, built, tested, and refined her robots.</p>"},{"id":"4899f845-7590-4dd1-be81-db87845941e7","content":"<p>Inspired by ants, which form bridges with their interlocked bodies to help fellow ants cross gaps, Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin set out to design quadruped robots capable of similarly collaborative behavior.</p>"},{"id":"cd915ebe-438d-493e-bc6f-267aa553e320","content":"<p>Ants, which have inspired the design of robots, form bridges by interlocking their bodies.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["4899f845-7590-4dd1-be81-db87845941e7"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence effectively begins a narrative about the creation of the robots, explaining that ants&rsquo; gap-crossing technique inspired Yasemin Ozkan-Aydin to \"set out to design\" robots that could collaborate in a similar manner.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes how the robots work together to cross gaps; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively begin a narrative about the creation of the robots. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence provides an overview of the steps Ozkan-Aydin took in creating the robots; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively begin a narrative about their creation. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence explains how ants form bridges; it doesn&rsquo;t effectively begin a narrative about the creation of the robots.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323048,"updateDate":1730147323048}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323048, 1730147323048),
    ($SATQ$4c43bf61$SATQ$, $SATQ$73bc7e8f-c451-4dc5-87bf-0d3615a8d4e7$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>The International Center for the Arts of the Americas (ICAA) is directed by Mari Carmen Ram&iacute;rez.</li>
<li>Ram&iacute;rez oversaw an initiative to create an online archive of historical documents related to the history of Latin American and Latino visual art.</li>
<li>The ICAA digitized over 10,000 documents, including the writings of Latin American and Latino artists and critics.</li>
<li>The creation of the archive didn&rsquo;t require historical documents to be removed from their countries of origin.</li>
<li>Scholars now have more access to these documents.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of the ICAA&rsquo;s archive being digital. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"4fe6acd4-5f79-467e-a519-eca2c44099e3","content":"<p>Over 10,000 documents related to the history of Latin American and Latino visual art are part of the ICAA archive.</p>"},{"id":"12d24014-818c-4994-8c82-b1a84d343830","content":"<p>By offering online versions of historical documents, the ICAA&rsquo;s archive provides more access to these materials without removing them from their countries of origin.</p>"},{"id":"679c4aae-f6b1-4ed4-a55b-a6fce62fbe01","content":"<p>Among the historical documents in the ICAA&rsquo;s archive are the writings of Latin American and Latino artists and critics.</p>"},{"id":"ddce5ab7-1f65-431b-810d-83b83bbdfbf4","content":"<p>The ICAA&rsquo;s director, Mari Carmen Ram&iacute;rez, oversaw the creation of an online archive of historical documents related to Latin American and Latino visual art.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["12d24014-818c-4994-8c82-b1a84d343830"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence explains an advantage of the ICAA&rsquo;s archive being digital, noting that the archive provides more access to historical documents since they don&rsquo;t have to be removed from their countries of origin.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the number of documents in the ICAA archive; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the archive being digital. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence notes the types of historical documents the ICAA&rsquo;s archive contains; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the archive being digital. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence identifies who oversaw the creation of the ICAA&rsquo;s online archive; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the archive being digital.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4c43bf61","external_id":"73bc7e8f-c451-4dc5-87bf-0d3615a8d4e7","vaultid":null,"uId":"0e8fde1d-7e3b-45b1-8f10-9f3dc422ead4","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The International Center for the Arts of the Americas (ICAA) is directed by Mari Carmen Ram&iacute;rez.</li>\n<li>Ram&iacute;rez oversaw an initiative to create an online archive of historical documents related to the history of Latin American and Latino visual art.</li>\n<li>The ICAA digitized over 10,000 documents, including the writings of Latin American and Latino artists and critics.</li>\n<li>The creation of the archive didn&rsquo;t require historical documents to be removed from their countries of origin.</li>\n<li>Scholars now have more access to these documents.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to explain an advantage of the ICAA&rsquo;s archive being digital. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"4fe6acd4-5f79-467e-a519-eca2c44099e3","content":"<p>Over 10,000 documents related to the history of Latin American and Latino visual art are part of the ICAA archive.</p>"},{"id":"12d24014-818c-4994-8c82-b1a84d343830","content":"<p>By offering online versions of historical documents, the ICAA&rsquo;s archive provides more access to these materials without removing them from their countries of origin.</p>"},{"id":"679c4aae-f6b1-4ed4-a55b-a6fce62fbe01","content":"<p>Among the historical documents in the ICAA&rsquo;s archive are the writings of Latin American and Latino artists and critics.</p>"},{"id":"ddce5ab7-1f65-431b-810d-83b83bbdfbf4","content":"<p>The ICAA&rsquo;s director, Mari Carmen Ram&iacute;rez, oversaw the creation of an online archive of historical documents related to Latin American and Latino visual art.</p>"}],"keys":["12d24014-818c-4994-8c82-b1a84d343830"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. The sentence explains an advantage of the ICAA&rsquo;s archive being digital, noting that the archive provides more access to historical documents since they don&rsquo;t have to be removed from their countries of origin.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes the number of documents in the ICAA archive; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the archive being digital. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence notes the types of historical documents the ICAA&rsquo;s archive contains; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the archive being digital. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence identifies who oversaw the creation of the ICAA&rsquo;s online archive; it doesn&rsquo;t explain an advantage of the archive being digital.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1730147323034,"updateDate":1730147323034}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1730147323034, 1730147323034),
    ($SATQ$4d2736f0$SATQ$, $SATQ$bcd3e56f-c9a6-4627-a402-65710bf36530$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$4b70fad3-f028-453a-ba89-5e693a9024f4$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>In her poetry collection <em>Thomas and Beulah</em>, Rita Dove interweaves the titular characters&rsquo; personal stories with broader historical narratives. She places Thomas&rsquo;s journey from the American South to the Midwest in the early 1900s within the larger context of the Great Migration. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> Dove sets events from Beulah&rsquo;s personal life against the backdrop of the US Civil Rights Movement.</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"cecbcd77-ff1f-4d55-860e-1152b00fc7d4","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"2eac6534-2aaa-4aff-acc6-9be9a2435d60","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"9ae874c2-8064-4af3-9c59-8dd4a4a43664","content":"<p>Regardless,</p>"},{"id":"3a051a2e-8acd-433f-99cb-f448fbf9416d","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3a051a2e-8acd-433f-99cb-f448fbf9416d"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Similarly&rdquo; logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that Dove situates Beulah&rsquo;s life in the context of the US Civil Rights Movement&mdash;is similar to the previous information about Thomas and the Great Migration. Both sentences support the first sentence&rsquo;s claim that Dove portrays her characters in the context of broader historical narratives.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Beulah in this sentence provides specific details elaborating on the previous information about Thomas. Instead, it&rsquo;s similar to the previous information about Thomas. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;thus&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Beulah in this sentence is a result or consequence of the previous information about Thomas. Instead, it&rsquo;s similar to the previous information about Thomas. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;regardless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Beulah in this sentence is true despite the previous information about Thomas. Instead, it&rsquo;s similar to the previous information about Thomas. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-223-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$f6f56604-7e8b-41bb-8255-48c44a9ebad4$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4d2736f0","external_id":"bcd3e56f-c9a6-4627-a402-65710bf36530","vaultid":"4b70fad3-f028-453a-ba89-5e693a9024f4","uId":"05821d83-810a-4991-8594-eaddab4557bd","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>In her poetry collection <em>Thomas and Beulah</em>, Rita Dove interweaves the titular characters&rsquo; personal stories with broader historical narratives. She places Thomas&rsquo;s journey from the American South to the Midwest in the early 1900s within the larger context of the Great Migration. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> Dove sets events from Beulah&rsquo;s personal life against the backdrop of the US Civil Rights Movement.</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"cecbcd77-ff1f-4d55-860e-1152b00fc7d4","content":"<p>Specifically,</p>"},{"id":"2eac6534-2aaa-4aff-acc6-9be9a2435d60","content":"<p>Thus,</p>"},{"id":"9ae874c2-8064-4af3-9c59-8dd4a4a43664","content":"<p>Regardless,</p>"},{"id":"3a051a2e-8acd-433f-99cb-f448fbf9416d","content":"<p>Similarly,</p>"}],"keys":["3a051a2e-8acd-433f-99cb-f448fbf9416d"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Similarly&rdquo; logically signals that the information in the sentence&mdash;that Dove situates Beulah&rsquo;s life in the context of the US Civil Rights Movement&mdash;is similar to the previous information about Thomas and the Great Migration. Both sentences support the first sentence&rsquo;s claim that Dove portrays her characters in the context of broader historical narratives.&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;specifically&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Beulah in this sentence provides specific details elaborating on the previous information about Thomas. Instead, it&rsquo;s similar to the previous information about Thomas. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;thus&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Beulah in this sentence is a result or consequence of the previous information about Thomas. Instead, it&rsquo;s similar to the previous information about Thomas. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;regardless&rdquo; illogically signals that the information about Beulah in this sentence is true despite the previous information about Thomas. Instead, it&rsquo;s similar to the previous information about Thomas. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-223-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"f6f56604-7e8b-41bb-8255-48c44a9ebad4","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959332,"updateDate":1691007959332}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959332, 1691007959332),
    ($SATQ$4e063114$SATQ$, $SATQ$5e176e34-def8-4aa6-8131-aac48549edf8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>
<ul>
<li>Georeferencing is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image.</li>
<li>This process enables mapping software to place the image in its real-world location.</li>
<li>A 2017 project by Tania L&oacute;pez Marrero and colleagues georeferenced a set of aerial photographs of Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline taken in 1930.</li>
<li>These photographs are the earliest known aerial photographs of Puerto Rico.</li>
<li>L&oacute;pez Marrero&rsquo;s project provided data that can help researchers analyze changes in Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to define the term &ldquo;georeferencing.&rdquo; Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"9def1f70-2861-4271-9d24-1e83666a83b8","content":"<p>A 2017 project by Tania L&oacute;pez Marrero and colleagues assigned geographic coordinates to photographs of Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline and also used georeferencing.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"ca759bb4-893b-4093-88ed-499726ee6f73","content":"<p>Tania L&oacute;pez Marrero and colleagues used georeferencing in their analysis of the earliest known aerial photographs of Puerto Rico.</p>"},{"id":"0939b9d0-117e-41eb-8b52-f446e7017b29","content":"<p>Georeferenced aerial photographs from 1930 can help researchers analyze changes in Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"521c9599-19a1-4ca1-804c-c658c3ba6bd9","content":"<p>Georeferencing is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image so that mapping software can place it in its real-world location.&nbsp;</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["521c9599-19a1-4ca1-804c-c658c3ba6bd9"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence defines the term "georeferencing," stating that it is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image so that mapping software can place the image in its real-world location.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes L&oacute;pez Marrero&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s 2017 project; it doesn&rsquo;t provide a definition of the term "georeferencing." Moreover, it misrepresents the information in the notes by characterizing the team&rsquo;s assignment of geographic coordinates to photographs as a process distinct from georeferencing. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence states that L&oacute;pez Marrero&rsquo;s team used georeferencing, but it doesn&rsquo;t provide a definition of the term. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence explains why a particular group of georeferenced aerial photographs is potentially useful for researchers; it doesn&rsquo;t provide a definition of the term "georeferencing."</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4e063114","external_id":"5e176e34-def8-4aa6-8131-aac48549edf8","vaultid":null,"uId":"706a3cfb-20db-4a0b-95d5-e4b2298cfde2","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:&nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp; &nbsp;</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Georeferencing is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image.</li>\n<li>This process enables mapping software to place the image in its real-world location.</li>\n<li>A 2017 project by Tania L&oacute;pez Marrero and colleagues georeferenced a set of aerial photographs of Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline taken in 1930.</li>\n<li>These photographs are the earliest known aerial photographs of Puerto Rico.</li>\n<li>L&oacute;pez Marrero&rsquo;s project provided data that can help researchers analyze changes in Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to define the term &ldquo;georeferencing.&rdquo; Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"9def1f70-2861-4271-9d24-1e83666a83b8","content":"<p>A 2017 project by Tania L&oacute;pez Marrero and colleagues assigned geographic coordinates to photographs of Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline and also used georeferencing.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"ca759bb4-893b-4093-88ed-499726ee6f73","content":"<p>Tania L&oacute;pez Marrero and colleagues used georeferencing in their analysis of the earliest known aerial photographs of Puerto Rico.</p>"},{"id":"0939b9d0-117e-41eb-8b52-f446e7017b29","content":"<p>Georeferenced aerial photographs from 1930 can help researchers analyze changes in Puerto Rico&rsquo;s coastline.&nbsp;</p>"},{"id":"521c9599-19a1-4ca1-804c-c658c3ba6bd9","content":"<p>Georeferencing is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image so that mapping software can place it in its real-world location.&nbsp;</p>"}],"keys":["521c9599-19a1-4ca1-804c-c658c3ba6bd9"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence defines the term \"georeferencing,\" stating that it is the process of assigning geographic coordinates to an image so that mapping software can place the image in its real-world location.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence describes L&oacute;pez Marrero&rsquo;s team&rsquo;s 2017 project; it doesn&rsquo;t provide a definition of the term \"georeferencing.\" Moreover, it misrepresents the information in the notes by characterizing the team&rsquo;s assignment of geographic coordinates to photographs as a process distinct from georeferencing. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence states that L&oacute;pez Marrero&rsquo;s team used georeferencing, but it doesn&rsquo;t provide a definition of the term. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence explains why a particular group of georeferenced aerial photographs is potentially useful for researchers; it doesn&rsquo;t provide a definition of the term \"georeferencing.\"</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669208,"updateDate":1743430669208}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669208, 1743430669208),
    ($SATQ$4f2710ab$SATQ$, $SATQ$e1d2bcfb-c4a8-43ee-aa27-c33539f9bc29$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$55199aca-f4c1-471d-b65c-4b03527dc50e$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$E$SATQ$, 3, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Organisms have evolved a number of surprising adaptations to ensure their survival in adverse conditions. Tadpole shrimp (<em>Triops longicaudatus</em>) embryos, <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> can pause development for over ten years during extended periods of drought.  </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"1b38ba63-2a3e-4635-b9d3-23b557c19ded","content":"<p>in contrast,</p>"},{"id":"1f161710-e224-423f-8fd8-1f7baea2d620","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"67dd2147-2cba-4451-86bc-9f5b5a4ae40b","content":"<p>meanwhile,</p>"},{"id":"ab186120-534b-4713-8bf1-9f14bc7a000a","content":"<p>consequently,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["B"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["1f161710-e224-423f-8fd8-1f7baea2d620"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;For example&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that tadpole shrimp embryos can pause development during extended periods of drought&mdash;exemplifies the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim that organisms have evolved surprising adaptations to survive in adverse conditions. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;in contrast&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the claim about organisms in the previous sentence. Instead, it exemplifies this claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is separate from (while occurring simultaneously with) the claim about organisms in the previous sentence. Instead, it exemplifies this claim. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;consequently&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a consequence, or result, of the claim about organisms in the previous sentence. Instead, it exemplifies this claim.&nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-224-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$3900bbff-5ebf-44a4-b807-b7ac0ed5b482$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4f2710ab","external_id":"e1d2bcfb-c4a8-43ee-aa27-c33539f9bc29","vaultid":"55199aca-f4c1-471d-b65c-4b03527dc50e","uId":"c5d632da-20f6-4934-abdd-4b27cb28c109","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"E","score_band_range_cd":3,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Organisms have evolved a number of surprising adaptations to ensure their survival in adverse conditions. Tadpole shrimp (<em>Triops longicaudatus</em>) embryos, <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> can pause development for over ten years during extended periods of drought.  </p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"1b38ba63-2a3e-4635-b9d3-23b557c19ded","content":"<p>in contrast,</p>"},{"id":"1f161710-e224-423f-8fd8-1f7baea2d620","content":"<p>for example,</p>"},{"id":"67dd2147-2cba-4451-86bc-9f5b5a4ae40b","content":"<p>meanwhile,</p>"},{"id":"ab186120-534b-4713-8bf1-9f14bc7a000a","content":"<p>consequently,</p>"}],"keys":["1f161710-e224-423f-8fd8-1f7baea2d620"],"correct_answer":["B"],"rationale":"<p>Choice B is the best answer. &ldquo;For example&rdquo; logically signals that the information in this sentence&mdash;that tadpole shrimp embryos can pause development during extended periods of drought&mdash;exemplifies the previous sentence&rsquo;s claim that organisms have evolved surprising adaptations to survive in adverse conditions. &nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;in contrast&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence contrasts with the claim about organisms in the previous sentence. Instead, it exemplifies this claim. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;meanwhile&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is separate from (while occurring simultaneously with) the claim about organisms in the previous sentence. Instead, it exemplifies this claim. Choice D is incorrect because &ldquo;consequently&rdquo; illogically signals that the information in this sentence is a consequence, or result, of the claim about organisms in the previous sentence. Instead, it exemplifies this claim.&nbsp;</p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-224-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"3900bbff-5ebf-44a4-b807-b7ac0ed5b482","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959332,"updateDate":1691007959332}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959332, 1691007959332),
    ($SATQ$4f9ee1dc$SATQ$, $SATQ$dd077789-490c-46b7-ac40-dbb44cb74dc8$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$99e4e339-eb9a-4d5a-865f-0b8907e5e668$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Seven species of sea turtle exist today.</li>
<li>Five sea turtle species can be found in the Atlantic Ocean.</li>
<li>One of those species is the Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle.</li>
<li>Its scientific name is <em>Lepidochelys kempii</em>.</li>
<li>Another of those species is the olive ridley sea turtle.</li>
<li>Its scientific name is <em>Lepidochelys olivacea</em>.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two sea turtle species. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal? </p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"36a794a0-e78c-466c-8b62-9add2dd6a734","content":"<p>Among the seven species of sea turtle is the olive ridley sea turtle, which can be found in the Atlantic Ocean.</p>"},{"id":"3bd55bb3-c589-4746-b19f-780cfd33a966","content":"<p>The Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle is referred to as <em>Lepidochelys kempii</em>, while the olive ridley sea turtle is referred to as <em>Lepidochelys olivacea</em>.</p>"},{"id":"3550c362-7ad5-4846-80d8-c94a6b436cf0","content":"<p>Both the Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle and the olive ridley sea turtle can be found in the Atlantic Ocean.</p>"},{"id":"127c2b08-dd8f-421c-8efa-7b1b64a203f6","content":"<p>The Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle (<em>Lepidochelys kempii</em>) and the olive ridley sea turtle (<em>Lepidochelys olivacea</em>) are different species.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["3550c362-7ad5-4846-80d8-c94a6b436cf0"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the two sea turtle species: both can be found in the Atlantic Ocean. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that the olive ridley sea turtle is one of seven species of sea turtle; it fails to mention the Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sea turtle species rather than a similarity. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sea turtle species rather than a similarity. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-735-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$7b668387-92c5-44c0-bb4f-0e0c6962ba2d$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4f9ee1dc","external_id":"dd077789-490c-46b7-ac40-dbb44cb74dc8","vaultid":"99e4e339-eb9a-4d5a-865f-0b8907e5e668","uId":"d8fec38d-52f4-4af7-8668-aea22a26dfc5","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Seven species of sea turtle exist today.</li>\n<li>Five sea turtle species can be found in the Atlantic Ocean.</li>\n<li>One of those species is the Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle.</li>\n<li>Its scientific name is <em>Lepidochelys kempii</em>.</li>\n<li>Another of those species is the olive ridley sea turtle.</li>\n<li>Its scientific name is <em>Lepidochelys olivacea</em>.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to emphasize a similarity between the two sea turtle species. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal? </p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"36a794a0-e78c-466c-8b62-9add2dd6a734","content":"<p>Among the seven species of sea turtle is the olive ridley sea turtle, which can be found in the Atlantic Ocean.</p>"},{"id":"3bd55bb3-c589-4746-b19f-780cfd33a966","content":"<p>The Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle is referred to as <em>Lepidochelys kempii</em>, while the olive ridley sea turtle is referred to as <em>Lepidochelys olivacea</em>.</p>"},{"id":"3550c362-7ad5-4846-80d8-c94a6b436cf0","content":"<p>Both the Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle and the olive ridley sea turtle can be found in the Atlantic Ocean.</p>"},{"id":"127c2b08-dd8f-421c-8efa-7b1b64a203f6","content":"<p>The Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle (<em>Lepidochelys kempii</em>) and the olive ridley sea turtle (<em>Lepidochelys olivacea</em>) are different species.</p>"}],"keys":["3550c362-7ad5-4846-80d8-c94a6b436cf0"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer. The sentence emphasizes a similarity between the two sea turtle species: both can be found in the Atlantic Ocean. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect. The sentence indicates that the olive ridley sea turtle is one of seven species of sea turtle; it fails to mention the Kemp&rsquo;s ridley sea turtle. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sea turtle species rather than a similarity. Choice D is incorrect. The sentence emphasizes a difference between the two sea turtle species rather than a similarity. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-735-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"7b668387-92c5-44c0-bb4f-0e0c6962ba2d","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959349,"updateDate":1691007959349}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959349, 1691007959349),
    ($SATQ$4fde4454$SATQ$, $SATQ$f52f87d7-f595-4af1-a549-5988359a6a38$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$000149fd-a7ec-480c-a6ed-e64933e89142$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$Transitions$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 5, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>One poll taken after the first 1960 presidential debate suggested that John Kennedy lost badly: only 21 percent of those who listened on the radio rated him the winner. <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span> the debate was ultimately considered a victory for the telegenic young senator, who rated higher than his opponent, Vice President Richard Nixon, among those watching on the new medium of television. &nbsp;</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"d2796a83-74fa-4ab6-a2e0-e2d6b04d4eee","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"},{"id":"1e04c794-c6b1-465a-ab97-96a755bcbc8f","content":"<p>Therefore,</p>"},{"id":"38545a1e-5de2-48e3-b267-8c76d58aa725","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"86dbc199-3160-4f1e-adbf-28538034812b","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["86dbc199-3160-4f1e-adbf-28538034812b"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Nevertheless&rdquo; logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that the telegenic Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner of the debate&mdash;is true despite the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;in other words&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is a paraphrase of the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. Instead, Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner despite what that poll suggested about his performance. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is a result of the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. Instead, Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner despite what that poll suggested about his performance. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. Instead, Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner despite what that poll suggested about his performance. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-207-TRA$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$9b672211-121f-40d7-9f8b-d4403c5c6ecf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"4fde4454","external_id":"f52f87d7-f595-4af1-a549-5988359a6a38","vaultid":"000149fd-a7ec-480c-a6ed-e64933e89142","uId":"a42fa775-3dc9-48ca-87c4-3c9f8fcdd75c","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"TRA","skill_desc":"Transitions","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>One poll taken after the first 1960 presidential debate suggested that John Kennedy lost badly: only 21 percent of those who listened on the radio rated him the winner. <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span> the debate was ultimately considered a victory for the telegenic young senator, who rated higher than his opponent, Vice President Richard Nixon, among those watching on the new medium of television. &nbsp;</p>","stem":"<p>Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"d2796a83-74fa-4ab6-a2e0-e2d6b04d4eee","content":"<p>In other words,</p>"},{"id":"1e04c794-c6b1-465a-ab97-96a755bcbc8f","content":"<p>Therefore,</p>"},{"id":"38545a1e-5de2-48e3-b267-8c76d58aa725","content":"<p>Likewise,</p>"},{"id":"86dbc199-3160-4f1e-adbf-28538034812b","content":"<p>Nevertheless,</p>"}],"keys":["86dbc199-3160-4f1e-adbf-28538034812b"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. &ldquo;Nevertheless&rdquo; logically signals that the claim in this sentence&mdash;that the telegenic Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner of the debate&mdash;is true despite the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. </p><p>Choice A is incorrect because &ldquo;in other words&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is a paraphrase of the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. Instead, Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner despite what that poll suggested about his performance. Choice B is incorrect because &ldquo;therefore&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is a result of the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. Instead, Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner despite what that poll suggested about his performance. Choice C is incorrect because &ldquo;likewise&rdquo; illogically signals that the claim in this sentence is similar to the previous information about the poll of radio listeners. Instead, Kennedy was ultimately considered the winner despite what that poll suggested about his performance. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-207-TRA","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"9b672211-121f-40d7-9f8b-d4403c5c6ecf","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959326,"updateDate":1691007959326}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959326, 1691007959326),
    ($SATQ$5222ffab$SATQ$, $SATQ$d01857e1-22bc-493b-b6d0-dd1a3127bdaf$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$acddef57-df5b-4411-b976-fd45c8d95280$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Expression of Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$EOI$SATQ$, $SATQ$SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$Rhetorical Synthesis$SATQ$, $SATQ$H$SATQ$, 6, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Neuroscientists Krishnan Padmanabhan and Zhen Chen sought to better understand the workings of the brain&rsquo;s olfactory system.</li>
<li>They devised a study using mathematical models.</li>
<li>They found that certain fibers allow the brain to toggle from one method of processing smells to another.</li>
<li>In one method, cells in the piriform cortex (where the perception of odor forms) capture olfactory information at a given moment.</li>
<li>In the other, the cells track changes in olfactory information over time.</li>
</ul>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>The student wants to summarize the study&rsquo;s findings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"07156946-954c-4dec-a023-bec05be78cc1","content":"<p>To arrive at these findings, which describe dual methods of processing smells in the piriform cortex, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study using mathematical models.</p>"},{"id":"74ce15ff-c5d5-4553-aa3d-8147249c6c8b","content":"<p>Padmanabhan and Chen showed that olfactory information is captured by cells in the piriform cortex, where the perception of odor forms.</p>"},{"id":"e8f5a8f3-7ccd-47af-ad20-9528d3e7140d","content":"<p>Using mathematical models, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study to better understand the workings of the brain&rsquo;s olfactory system.</p>"},{"id":"6cea4c66-4a33-4f69-9405-ab9d5b396f45","content":"<p>According to Padmanabhan and Chen, the brain can toggle between capturing olfactory information at a given moment and tracking changes in that information over time.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["D"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["6cea4c66-4a33-4f69-9405-ab9d5b396f45"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence effectively summarizes the study&rsquo;s findings, explaining what Padmanabhan and Chen found: that the brain can toggle between one method of processing smells (capturing information at a given moment) and another (tracking changes in information over time).&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence mentions findings, it mainly focuses on Padmanabhan and Chen&rsquo;s methodology. It doesn&rsquo;t effectively summarize the study&rsquo;s findings. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence notes a fact about the olfactory system&mdash;that the perception of odor forms in the piriform cortex&mdash;but doesn&rsquo;t summarize the findings of Padmanabhan and Chen&rsquo;s study. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence presents the goal of Padmanabhan and Chen&rsquo;s study; it doesn&rsquo;t summarize the study&rsquo;s findings. </p>$SATQ$, false, 0, $SATQ$OSP-034-SYN$SATQ$, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$30423779-b680-405e-b608-7a8b44ad82a0$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$$SATQ$, $SATQ$proteus$SATQ$, $SATQ$0$SATQ$, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"5222ffab","external_id":"d01857e1-22bc-493b-b6d0-dd1a3127bdaf","vaultid":"acddef57-df5b-4411-b976-fd45c8d95280","uId":"8e223bac-70e4-4f89-bb85-1982fe57a189","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"EOI","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill_cd":"SYN","skill_desc":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"H","score_band_range_cd":6,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Neuroscientists Krishnan Padmanabhan and Zhen Chen sought to better understand the workings of the brain&rsquo;s olfactory system.</li>\n<li>They devised a study using mathematical models.</li>\n<li>They found that certain fibers allow the brain to toggle from one method of processing smells to another.</li>\n<li>In one method, cells in the piriform cortex (where the perception of odor forms) capture olfactory information at a given moment.</li>\n<li>In the other, the cells track changes in olfactory information over time.</li>\n</ul>","stem":"<p>The student wants to summarize the study&rsquo;s findings. Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"07156946-954c-4dec-a023-bec05be78cc1","content":"<p>To arrive at these findings, which describe dual methods of processing smells in the piriform cortex, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study using mathematical models.</p>"},{"id":"74ce15ff-c5d5-4553-aa3d-8147249c6c8b","content":"<p>Padmanabhan and Chen showed that olfactory information is captured by cells in the piriform cortex, where the perception of odor forms.</p>"},{"id":"e8f5a8f3-7ccd-47af-ad20-9528d3e7140d","content":"<p>Using mathematical models, Padmanabhan and Chen devised a study to better understand the workings of the brain&rsquo;s olfactory system.</p>"},{"id":"6cea4c66-4a33-4f69-9405-ab9d5b396f45","content":"<p>According to Padmanabhan and Chen, the brain can toggle between capturing olfactory information at a given moment and tracking changes in that information over time.</p>"}],"keys":["6cea4c66-4a33-4f69-9405-ab9d5b396f45"],"correct_answer":["D"],"rationale":"<p>Choice D is the best answer. The sentence effectively summarizes the study&rsquo;s findings, explaining what Padmanabhan and Chen found: that the brain can toggle between one method of processing smells (capturing information at a given moment) and another (tracking changes in information over time).&nbsp;</p><p>Choice A is incorrect. While the sentence mentions findings, it mainly focuses on Padmanabhan and Chen&rsquo;s methodology. It doesn&rsquo;t effectively summarize the study&rsquo;s findings. Choice B is incorrect. The sentence notes a fact about the olfactory system&mdash;that the perception of odor forms in the piriform cortex&mdash;but doesn&rsquo;t summarize the findings of Padmanabhan and Chen&rsquo;s study. Choice C is incorrect. The sentence presents the goal of Padmanabhan and Chen&rsquo;s study; it doesn&rsquo;t summarize the study&rsquo;s findings. </p>","parenttemplatename":"OSP-034-SYN","parenttemplateid":"","templateclusterid":"30423779-b680-405e-b608-7a8b44ad82a0","templateclustername":"","origin":"proteus","position":"0","createDate":1691007959320,"updateDate":1691007959320}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1691007959320, 1691007959320)
